WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
HANK: Good morning, John!

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:03.659
I live in a city that most people would call
a small town.

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:06.610
Missoula, Montana has 70,000 people in it
when the students are here, and when they

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:10.090
leave for the summer, the population declines
significantly.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.930
Somehow, 70,000 people is considered to be
a small number of people.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.840
It actually is quite a large number of people,
but, I mean, after all there are some cities

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.870
that have stadiums that hold more than 70,000
people, so.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:24.350
For some perspective, Missoula packs those
70,000 people into 2600 square miles.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:29.740
Manhattan has 22 times more people in 78 times
less area.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.920
And, of course, there are many amazing things
going on in Manhattan.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.769
It isn't just 1.3 million people.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:42.070
It's 1.3 million of the richest, most powerful,
most influential people on the planet.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.510
Economic and cultural creation on the scale
of Renaissance Italy.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:49.629
And that is an interesting comparison because
Florence in 1500 contained some of the most

00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:54.600
powerful economic and cultural forces the
world has ever known.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.170
The Medici bankers, Botticelli, Machiavelli,
Michaelangelo, da Vinci, Brunelleschi.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:03.000
It revolutionized the world from the seat
of the Renaissance in Florence, Italy.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
And what was the population of Florence, Italy?

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.430
1.3 million people?

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:08.430
No.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.780
No millions of people!

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.610
No hundreds of thousands of people!

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.610
70,000.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.270
Of course there were tremendous political
and economic and cultural and historic forces

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.430
that made Florence the seat of the Renaissance.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.890
But one thing that I believe very deeply in
my heart, and I think most historians would

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:28.770
agree with me, is that it didn't happen because
there was just some random collection of really

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.310
great people.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.560
Great people are everywhere all the time.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.929
It just so happens that only occasionally
our cultural and historical and economic forces,

00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:42.330
such that they will be remembered for hundreds
or even thousands of years.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:46.320
So I like to think of Missoula as like a little
Florence.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.490
And everyone walking around is an unnoticed
da Vinci and an unmade Machiavelli.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.170
And I think about what separates the great
people that I remember from the great people

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.600
that everyone remembers.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.570
I think it's 100% circumstance.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:04.060
We're launching a new channel this week with
Jessi Knudsen Castañeda and her husband Augusto,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.920
who you may know from their frequent appearances
on SciShow.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.180
They bring to us animals that they have rescued
so that we can talk about them and educate

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.180
the world.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.180
That's Jessi's passion!

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.180
That's her superpower.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.790
And Caitlin Hoffmeister, who's also the producer
of SciShow, is going to be documenting the

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:25.030
passion, and sweat, and toil that it takes
to run a small business and feed hundreds

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.030
of mouths.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.960
Two of those mouths happen to be twin infant
children.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.330
Now that I've produced shows with a bunch
of different interesting people here in Missoula--Emily

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:37.780
Graslie, and Lindsey Doe, and now Jessi--people
have asked me why are there so many interesting

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.830
things going on in this one little small town
in Missoula?

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:41.830
That's the thing, though!

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:42.830
That's the point!

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.090
There's interetsting things happening everywhere!

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.170
This isn't a magical place and they're not
magical people.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:50.340
It's just people who have found something
that they're passionate about.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.310
But yeah, that can be a hard thing to come
by.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.940
I've watched everyone I've ever known struggle
with that.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.470
Trying to find something that they believe
is important.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:04.640
And I'm so grateful--changing topics here,
really fast--that Nerdfighteria has provided

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.640
that for me.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.350
People talk about all the things that I've
been able to do.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:12.120
But it is only because I found something here
that I can believe in.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.040
Because, yeah, I live in Missoula, Montana,
but my first allegiance is to this place.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.450
A community of hundreds of thousands of people
with shared ideas, and values and interests.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:26.890
Bigger than Missoula and thus even more full
of unappreciated and unnoticed da Vincis and

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.560
Machiavellis and Brunelleschis.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.570
The only difference between something being
vitally important, and not important at all,

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.300
is whether or not you believe it is.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.520
I believe that this is important, and that
is a gift that you have given me.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.520
Thank you!

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.860
John, your life seems like a bit of a grapefruit
right now.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.430
Don't freak out.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.800
Uhh, stay sane.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.100
And I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.860
If you want to check out Jessi's new channel,
there's a link in the description.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.720
And there's also you can just click over here
if you're not on your phone, you can click

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:53.720
with your mouse.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:54.720
But not with your phone, it doesn't work on
phones.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:54.819
Boop.

